# Environment for the control server. Copy to snipe.env (gitignored) and fill in.
#
#   cp snipe.env.example snipe.env
#   nano snipe.env
#   chmod 600 snipe.env
#
# Then start the server with:
#   set -a && source snipe.env && set +a && npm run snipe:server

# ── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The only thing standing between the internet and your wallets. Generate one:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
SNIPE_TOKEN=

# ── Wallet protection ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Encrypts the key file on disk (AES-256-GCM, scrypt). Protects backups,
# snapshots and file reads; does not protect against root on the live box —
# nothing that signs unattended can. Pick something long; to change it later:
#   SNIPE_KEYSTORE_NEW_PASSPHRASE='new one' npm run keys:rekey -- snipe.config.json
# SNIPE_KEYSTORE_PASSPHRASE=

# Where ETH may leave to, instantly. Comma-separated 0x addresses. Anything
# else — collecting to an unknown address — is refused by the signing policy.
# Addresses can also be registered over the API, but those wait an hour.
# SNIPE_WITHDRAW_TO=0xYourMainWallet

# The most ETH one mint transaction may carry (default 0.05). Stops a fake
# expensive "drop" from draining wallets through a legitimate-looking mint.
# SNIPE_POLICY_MAX_MINT_ETH=0.05

# ── Split the keys into their own process (for the multi-user build) ─────────
# Run two pm2 processes: `signerd` holds the keys and the passphrase and
# serves signatures on this socket; the API is started WITHOUT the passphrase
# and only knows this path. A break-in in the API can then ask for a signature
# the policy allows but cannot read or carry off a wallet.
#
#   signerd:  SNIPE_KEYSTORE_PASSPHRASE + SNIPE_SIGNER_SOCKET   (has the keys)
#   API:      SNIPE_SIGNER_SOCKET only, NO passphrase           (holds none)
#
# Leave it unset for a single process that signs in-process, as before.
# SNIPE_SIGNER_SOCKET=/home/ubuntu/launchsoft/snipe.signer.sock

# ── Telegram summaries (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Token from @BotFather (/newbot). Chat id: message your bot once, then open
#   https://api.telegram.org/bot<TOKEN>/getUpdates
# and read result[0].message.chat.id
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=

# ── Optional tuning ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SNIPE_PORT=8787
# SNIPE_HOST=127.0.0.1
# Restrict which site may drive this server (default "*" is fine behind a
# tunnel + token, but narrowing costs nothing):
# SNIPE_ORIGINS=https://launchpad-44c.pages.dev
# How far ahead of a stage a job is armed, in ms:
# SNIPE_ARM_LEAD_MS=120000

# The endpoints this box works through, best first. Comma or newline
# separated. Without it every read goes to the chain's public RPC, which
# meters — a scan then spends its time being rate-limited and split into ever
# smaller pieces. The panel can also push an endpoint down, but that depends
# on which browser last visited; this does not.
#
# Set just this one and everything uses it. The two below carve out the jobs
# worth keeping apart, and each falls back to this when unset.
# SNIPE_RPCS=https://robinhood-mainnet.g.alchemy.com/v2/YOUR_KEY
#
# Arming and broadcasting a queued mint. The broadcast races every endpoint
# at once with the sequencer first, so this is one racer among several — but
# the arming is not: a hundred wallets is a hundred balance reads and a
# hundred nonce reads down this one endpoint, two minutes before the stage
# opens. A sweep landing on the same key at that moment is what loses a drop.
# SNIPE_MINT_RPCS=https://robinhood-mainnet.g.alchemy.com/v2/YOUR_SNIPE_KEY
#
# The scanner, the live feed, the calendar and the profit report. These want
# wide eth_getLogs ranges — an hour of this chain is around 35,000 blocks —
# and most free plans cap that hard. Alchemy's free tier allows ten blocks,
# which is not a scanner endpoint at any price. The chain's own RPC has no
# such cap; a paid plan lifts it. The server probes this at startup and says
# in the log if the endpoint cannot serve a scan.
# SNIPE_SCAN_RPCS=https://robinhood-mainnet.g.alchemy.com/v2/YOUR_SCAN_KEY

# How many connections may be held open to each endpoint, warmed before a
# stage so nothing negotiates TLS at the moment it fires. The default (4096)
# is well clear of any realistic wallet count; it exists because the old
# ceiling of 512 silently clamped a thousand-wallet blast and left 488 wallets
# shaking hands at T-0. The runner says so in its log if it ever clamps again.
# SNIPE_MAX_SOCKETS=4096
#
# Every warmed connection is a file descriptor, and a fresh Ubuntu box allows
# 1024 — which a few hundred wallets across three endpoints walks straight
# through. The runner warns when the wallet count approaches the limit. To
# raise it for the service, add to the pm2 unit (or the shell that starts it):
#   ulimit -n 65536

# ── Drop index ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The scanner used to re-read the chain on every visit: three days of logs is
# six million blocks, it is the heaviest thing this server does, and it put a
# ceiling on how far back you could look — thirty days takes about ninety
# seconds, and a Cloudflare tunnel gives up on a request before that.
#
# So a worker keeps a table of drops on disk beside the config, reading only
# the blocks since its last pass (around 600 a minute) and walking backwards in
# steps until it covers the window. The scanner then answers from that table,
# at once, however far back it asks.
#
# Measured on this chain: catching up thirty days costs roughly 14,000 compute
# units once, and keeping up about 500,000 a month — against the 22 million a
# month the scanner's auto-refresh already spends. About 52,000 rows and 10 MB
# for thirty days.
#
# Set SNIPE_INDEX=0 to turn it off; scans then read the chain per request and
# the 14d and 30d windows go away.
# SNIPE_INDEX=0
# SNIPE_INDEX_DAYS=30
# SNIPE_INDEX_POLL_MS=60000
# How many blocks one catch-up step reads — about two hours of this chain:
# SNIPE_INDEX_STEP_BLOCKS=72000

# How the blast is split into waves.
#
# Node has one thread, so a single synchronous pass queues every request and
# the write for the first wallet does not reach the kernel until the pass is
# over. Every wallet you load therefore slows down wallet number one — the one
# that actually wins the drop. Measured on a four-core box against three local
# endpoints, first wallet away: 22 wallets 9.6ms, 300 wallets 93ms.
#
# So the first SNIPE_WAVE_SIZE wallets fire on their own, at the sequencer
# alone — on a first-come-first-served chain nothing else decides ordering, and
# the other endpoints in that wave are only competition for the thread. The
# rest follow once the head has answered, and the head is then repeated to the
# endpoints it skipped, so every wallet still reaches every endpoint.
#
#   300 wallets, no waves   first away 93ms     everything 197ms
#   300 wallets, head 100   first away 13.3ms   everything 163ms
#    22 wallets, no waves   first away 9.6ms    everything  24ms
#    22 wallets, head 100   first away 3.6ms    everything  16ms
#
# SNIPE_WAVE_GAP_MS is a ceiling, not a delay: the second wave leaves as soon
# as the first has answered, and the cap only stops a dead endpoint from
# holding the rest of the blast. Set SNIPE_WAVE_SIZE=0 to turn the split off
# and fire everything at every endpoint at once, as before.
#
# The numbers above are from a four-core box. Measure your own — the table
# below them is what "npm run blast:bench" prints, on the machine that fires.
# SNIPE_WAVE_SIZE=100
# SNIPE_WAVE_GAP_MS=40

# How many chain reads may be in flight at once. Arming a wallet set is two
# reads per wallet — balance and nonce — and they all happen two minutes
# before a stage opens, so this is what decides whether a large set arms in
# seconds or in minutes. Measured over 300 balance reads: the chain's public
# RPC managed 5/second at the old default of 25 (with 220 rate-limit
# backoffs); Alchemy on a paid plan managed 1045/second at 200. Default 100.
# SNIPE_READ_CONCURRENCY=200

